Good Day,

I have been "playing" with my data and Distance for while, trying to impr=
ove=20
my analyses as I learn more from the book and the material from the=20
workshop. But I came across a problem that I cannot figure out.

Here a summary of what I did (I will try to be concise):

aerial survey, systematic line-transect
2 independent observers on each side of the plane
2 years of data

Question of interest: density and abundance for each year

I ran MCDS and tested 4 covariates of which only observer had an effect o=
n=20
the detection function. So I post-stratified by observer and since I want=
ed to=20
have estimates for each year separately, I used the "data selection" to l=
ook at=20
each year individually. So I ran 2 analysis (1 for each year). For each a=
nalysis,=20
D and N were computed for each observer and for both observer pooled (mea=
n=20
weighted by total effort in the stratum).

But then, I realised that since the detection function was the same betwe=
en=20
years for each observer, it would probably be better to estimate a pooled=
 f(0)=20
over the 2 years for each observer and used the pooled f(0) to compute th=
e=20
density and abundance estimates for each year and each observer.
The only way I managed to do this was to post-stratify by year and then=20=

select for each observer. So I ran 2 analysis (1 for each observer). For =
each=20
analysis, D and N were computed for each year but with a pooled f(0) over=
=20
the 2 years. Then, to obtain overall (both observers combined) estimates =
of D=20
and N for each year, I ran an analysis with year as a stratum and with no=
 data=20
selection.=20

My thoughs were this latter option was a better approach but I am a bit=20=

concerned because the estimates are different and, more importantly, have=
 a=20
higher CV than with the first method. I am not totally sure I understand =
why is=20
that. For what I understood, in the first option the pooled estimates are=
=20
weighted means whereas the second option computed estimates from the=20
data of both observers combined, so that would explain why the values are=
=20
different and I would tend to think that the second option is better. But=
 I still=20
don't understand why the CV would be increased (8.51 vs. 6.87 and 10.41 v=
s.=20
9.56).

I really greatly appreciate your insigth on this matter.

Thank you,

Magaly

